From 0d9b1e8aed99fc665b54cf2b94ec5b8a4c259041 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sharply Contributor <sharply-dev@example.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 07:32:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Use real per-camera calibration matrices for RAW color

decode_raw was calling raw.cam_to_xyz_normalized(), which relies on
RawImage::xyz_to_cam - a field rawler never actually populates for any
camera. Every RAW was silently falling back to naive as-shot sensor
RGB with no real camera color science applied.

rawler does ship genuine per-camera-model calibration data (the same
database Adobe's DNG Converter uses) in RawImage::color_matrix, keyed
by calibration illuminant - typically one tungsten ('A') and one
daylight ('D65') matrix per camera. This switches to that data:

- Selects and, when a camera has two calibration illuminants, blends
  them in mired space based on this shot's own as-shot white balance
  (the same approach DNG-based converters use), instead of one fixed
  transform.
- Estimates scene color temperature via McCamy's formula, with a
  short fixed-point refinement.
- Falls back to as-shot sensor RGB only when a camera truly has no
  calibration entry at all.
- Surfaces calibration status/estimated Kelvin in the returned
  profile string.
- Expands per-make look presets (saturation/contrast) beyond
  Canon/Nikon/Sony to Fuji, Olympus/OM, Panasonic, Pentax, Leica,
  Kodak, Samsung, Apple and medium-format bodies.

Verified the matrix math (inversion, mired blending, CCT estimation)
against a standalone harness; the real crate needs a newer Rust
toolchain than was available to compile end-to-end here.
